-  "levels_won_this_session": [5],
-  "novel_mechanics_found": "L5 begins behind a completed-level transition frame: one further action is required to reveal it. Its 6x6 c/d stack cycles through one additional c-row on each directional action (period 6); approaching the stack at the correct spacing freezes a phase, and the next push launches the six-row object over the ceiling. A dynamic 3x3 modulo glyph beside the socket encodes the stack's c-row count modulo 3. The glyph itself is ballistic: push it left to clear the socket, then move the selected e-framed 3x3 block left into the vacated socket to win. L6 adds phase-dependent transport: striking the lower cycling stack can route the selected block through a six-row wall into an isolated upper chamber while leaving the stack behind.",
-  "narrative": "Replayed the resolver's 201-action prefix from a fresh reset and confirmed frame.levels_completed == 4. After probing the six stack phases, I froze and extracted the L5 stack, navigated the selected block around the ceiling, launched the derived glyph left, and moved the block into its socket; the live frame advanced to 5 at action 234. I continued through action 293 on L6. The chosen lower-stack phase routed the block into the upper-right chamber, where I filled the square socket, but the three-pixel void strips made that chamber one-way and left the plus-shaped object isolated in the upper-left chamber. With no observable escape from that branch, I stopped and ran reproduce() on the 234-action prefix; it reached level 5 with reproduced=true.",
+  "levels_won_this_session": [
+    6
+  ],
+  "novel_mechanics_found": "L6 lower-stack transport is not a fixed one-way trap: the six-row cycling stack is itself ballistic. Launching it left twice relocates the elevator from x=42 to x=12; phase-0 alignment then sends the selected 3x3 block into the upper-left chamber. After the plus is solved, launching the stack right twice restores the route to the upper-right square socket. L7 generalizes the transport to different block aspect ratios: a 3x6 and a 6x3 selectable block have distinct sockets, both can ride the lower stack across the lower barrier, and only the narrow block fits the central three-cell gap.",
+  "narrative": "The resolver prefix was independently replayed from a fresh reset and confirmed at levels_completed == 5 after 234 steps. A second persistent replay reached the same state, after which I discovered that relocating the L6 lower stack left opens the supposedly isolated plus chamber; I solved the plus with backtrack-clamped left/up launches, moved the stack back right, and placed the selected square to observe levels_completed advance live to 6 at exploratory action 124. I continued for 100 actions on L7, socketing both rectangular objects and mapping its lower transport and central gap, but did not solve the sealed plus chamber. A fresh level-6 reproduction gate could not fit after the two required 234-step replays without exceeding the 700-step cap, so the live win is reported honestly as unverified.",
   "gotchas": [
-    "The frame returned by the L4 winning action is still the completed L4 board even though levels_completed is already 4; one additional action reveals L5.",
-    "Directional actions advance L5/L6 color phases, but ineffective ACTION6 clicks do not; clicks can therefore hold a phase while consuming an env.step call.",
-    "A first contact with the cycling L5 stack freezes/arms it and a subsequent push performs the long extraction; apparent blocked approaches can be phase-sensitive.",
-    "In L5 the 3x3 c/d glyph begins immediately beside and partly over the socket border. Launch it left first; the selected e block then needs only the final leftward move into the cleared socket.",
-    "L6 contains three-pixel color-2 void strips, not walkable corridors. The selected 3x3 block cannot cross them or contact the upper cycling stacks through them.",
-    "L6 lower-stack transport is phase/direction sensitive and can be one-way. The phase used in this run sent the block to the upper-right chamber before the left plus target was solved, which appears to be an unrecoverable ordering trap."
+    "This session used 234 steps for the mandatory independent prefix confirmation and another 234 to establish a persistent exploratory environment; only 232 calls remained, making a 358-step fresh reproduction gate impossible within the 700-call cap.",
+    "In L6 the lower cycling stack can be launched horizontally in 15-pixel flights. Its x-position determines which upper chamber a phase-0 alignment reaches; leave the square target until after the plus is solved.",
+    "A selected block aligned above a cycling stack transports across the barrier when the stack reaches its all-d phase (phase 0); blocked directional moves still advance phase, while ineffective ACTION6 clicks do not.",
+    "For L6, launch the plus left first so backtrack-clamp places it at x=3, then launch it upward into the target; solve order matters because the square socket is in the other chamber.",
+    "L7 has two selectable e-framed blocks. ACTION6 at their rendered centers switches selection and restores the previously selected block interior to its underlying target color.",
+    "L7 shape width is load-bearing: the 6x3 block cannot use the three-column central gap, whereas the 3x6 block can. The color-15 strips enclosing the plus chamber remained unexplained at stop time."
   ],
   "solve_provenance": "live_agent_self_discovery"
 }
